{
  "comment": "Targeted contract for GET /api/firmware, the read-only firmware inventory (docs/refactoring-policy.md). The deployment routes (/api/firmware-files, -inspect, -deploy, -options, -stop, -status, -deploy-status) are not part of this set and are never called. Runs in the isolated container: the only network interface is lo. Printers: 0 Alpha (everyone), 1 Bravo (grp_staff only), 2 Charlie (offline, nothing listening); all klipper-moonraker, which declares firmwareInfo but NOT firmwareDeploy. Users: admin; viewer (no groups = Everyone). So the LIST is empty for everyone here (no fixture printer is flashable): the JSON answer is [] and the stream is exactly 2 records (initial, done) — list rows, batching and the per-row decoration are pinned in-process only (test/firmwareInventoryBehaviour.test.js). The single-printer read (?printer=i) does not apply eligibility and reaches the fake printer through connectors/http-utils.js queryFirmwareInfo: GET /printer/info and /machine/system_info (both 404 on the fake unless switched on), /printer/objects/list and /printer/objects/query?mcu; those requests are compared like every other side effect. One read runs with Alpha told to answer /machine/system_info (product and distribution fields) for that step only; it is switched back afterwards. Every request declares the status the fixture must produce (\"expect\"), and the stream its record count; a miss, or a control step the fake printer does not acknowledge, is an error of the run. Nothing here is declared volatile. trackDelays: every single read of a printer that answers creates one 20 s timer (FW_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS) and clears it; the lifecycle probe records runtime timers only for declared delays, so 20000 is declared here and its created/cleared/fired counts are compared per process start.",
  "trackDelays": [
    20000
  ],
